Crown Mountain Resources Ltd. is proposing to construct and operate an open-pit metallurgical coal mine in the Elk Valley, approximately 12&nbsp;kilometres from the District of Sparwood in southeastern British Columbia. As proposed, the production capacity of the project would be approximately 2.0 million tonnes per year, over a mine-life of about 15 years.

&nbsp;

Please note that the proponent for this project was previously NWP Coal Canada Ltd.

The proposed project consists of constructing a paved Ground Support Equipment (GSE) parking and staging area on previously developed airport lands adjacent to the terminal building and existing apron. The work includes pavement construction, grading, stormwater management infrastructure, subdrain installation, utility servicing for future electric GSE charging infrastructure, lighting modifications, and associated site works. The project will support airport ground operations, improve equipment storage and circulation. The total expansion area is approximately 2,200 m2.
&nbsp;
During construction, stormwater runoff and sediment control measures will be provided by the site contractor, as required, and reviewed by the Airport Authority on a regular basis. All municipal, provincial and federal codes, standards, and best management practices will be followed. The expansion is within previously developed areas (i.e., existing GSE area or landscaped area) with no woodland or known sensitive ecological habitat being impacted.

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to install 100 meters of 60 millimeter diameter polyethylene gas main and 65 meters of 42 millimeter gas service line at Blood Tribe. The new distribution gas main will accommodate a proposed new administration building in Standoff adjacent to the Kainai Wellness Center. The proposed gas line will be installed at a depth of 0.8 to 1.2 meters and will be tied into the existing gas main. The proposed methods of installation will include trenching, as well as horizontal directional drilling and use of hydrovac when required.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling, re-grading, reclamation, and flaring of the gas to ensure the gas line has 100 percent gas in it.

&nbsp;

No vegetation clearing is proposed and the project is not located within 30 meters of any waterbodies or wetlands. Wildlife species at risk are not anticipated to be present in the project construction area. A wildlife and nest sweep will be completed prior to construction.

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to install 830 meters of 60 millimeter diameter polyethylene gas main and six short 42 millimeter gas services at Blood Tribe on previously cleared lands. The new distribution gas main will accommodate a proposed commercial subdivision on the eastern edge of Standoff. The proposed gas line will be installed at a depth of 0.8 to 1.2 meters and will be tied into the existing gas main. The proposed methods of installation will include trenching, as well as horizontal directional drilling and use of hydrovac when required.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling, re-grading, reclamation, and flaring of the gas to ensure the gas line has 100 percent gas in it.

&nbsp;

No vegetation clearing is proposed and the project is not located near any waterbodies or wetlands. Wildlife species at risk are not anticipated to be present in the project construction area. A wildlife and nest sweep will be completed prior to construction.

The Proposed Project: Laydown and Logistics areas&nbsp;

As part of the Greater Toronto Airports Authority&#39;s (GTAA&#39;s) Toronto Pearson Airport Longterm Investment in Facilities &amp; Terminals (LIFT) Program,&nbsp;logistics&nbsp;and laydown areas will be constructed on Airport Lands, to&nbsp;facilitate&nbsp;construction needs.&nbsp;These sites will be developed on&nbsp;unmaintained&nbsp;vegetated&nbsp;areas&nbsp;located&nbsp;both airside and groundside&nbsp;on&nbsp;airport&nbsp;property.&nbsp;The sites will consist of&nbsp;a combination of any of the following:&nbsp;temporary construction trailers, worker parking lot, material laydown, equipment storage, and associated infrastructure to support Airport construction projects.&nbsp;

The sites will undergo a variety of improvements, depending on&nbsp;the current conditions and&nbsp;the requirements for construction.&nbsp;Activities may include, as&nbsp;required,&nbsp;vegetation removal,&nbsp;minor excavation, clearing and grubbing, utility removals /relocations including the addition of temporary pole-mounted lighting with overhead power, re-grading , placement of aggregate and compact areas where required, construction trailers, security booths, transportation services for workers, paving and line painting of parking and trailer areas, stormwater management, and other associated infrastructure. No new permanent buildings are proposed. Trailers will be prefabricated units delivered to the site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Several sustainability best practices will be incorporated into the projects design and implementation for the wider Toronto Pearson Airport Longterm Investment in Facilities &amp; Terminals (LIFT) Program.

This is a project to demolish the solar panels and associated piping planned for the fall/winter 2019.

The project involves removing the six sections of ground mounted solar panels and all associated piping, valves, fittings and related mechanical items, removing the concrete piers, removing the chain link fence around the site, removing a set of stairs and landing, and removing a glycol tank and piping within the Central Heating Plant. The existing pipe bridge connecting the solar panel system to the Central Heating Plant building will remain. The gravel throughout the site will remain and topsoil will be placed on top and hydro seeded.

&nbsp;

Pikangikum First Nation requires upgrades and repairs to existing water and wastewater infrastructure with short-term work being conducted in a two-phased approach. The Water Treatment Plant is over 30 years old, in poor condition, and under capacity to meet community demands. Similarily, the Wastewater Treatment System is older and in poor condition.

Phase 1: Immediate upgrades to the Water Treatment Plant will include replacement of existing mechanical and electrical components to ensure the systems remain operational with no increased capacity or improvement to treatment/automation. The Wastewater system needs replacement of existing components to ensure operations and improve the working conditions (i.e., health and safety) for operators.&nbsp;

Phase 2: Interim upgrades include the design and installation of a new containerized&nbsp; water treatment system with membrane filtration (10 Litres per second) adjacent to the existing Water Treatment Plant which will be connected to the community&#39;s water reservoir and distribution system. The Wastewater system will also have design and installation of a new containerized wastewater treatment system (Moving Bed Biofilm Reactor) which will receive wastewater from a new sewage pumping station, re-routed forcemain and a new maintenance hole.&nbsp;This work will result in moderate increased capacity with a significant improvement in treatment, redundancy, and automation of both the Water and Wastewater Systems.&nbsp;

Longer term recommendations including design and construction of new infrastructure and distribution and collection systems are estimated to take approximately five to seven years following the completion of the phase 1 and 2 works described above.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Proposed Project&nbsp;

This project involves the of the renovation and expansion of the Airport Firehall. Site preparation activities will include geo-technical studies, surveys, and environmental studies.

The Saskatoon Airport Authority is the leaseholder for this&nbsp;development on its property and is therefore the lead authority on this project.&nbsp;

Environmental description includes greenfield surrounded by industrial/commercial land.&nbsp;

The activities assessed are part of a larger&nbsp;project&nbsp;which is funded by the International Development Research Centre and implemented by&nbsp;the World Resources Institute Indonesia (WRI Indonesia)&nbsp;which&nbsp;aims to identify the most cost-effective, gender-responsive and nature-friendly climate-smart strategies for increasing production of small-scale shrimp aquaculture while simultaneously restoring the critical mangrove ecosystems.

The project will be implemented in collaboration with shrimp farmers, coastal communities, government, supply chain companies, the private&nbsp;sector&nbsp;and other stakeholders.&nbsp;The&nbsp;larger&nbsp;project aims to generate knowledge on climate-smart processes among producers; implement a pilot study utilizing the climate-smart strategies; share results with internet-based, real-time data; strengthen capacities of stakeholders to implement the new strategies and mangrove restoration; and facilitate policymakers&#39; active use of the evidence to drive policies and interventions in the aquaculture sector.&nbsp;

As a part of the larger project, the proponent&nbsp;will&nbsp;&nbsp;implement&nbsp;a Communal Wastewater Treatment Plant (WWTP),&nbsp;which includes sedimentation plots, an aeration pond, and a wetland pond. &nbsp;

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to relocate approximately 500m of 60.3mm diameter polyethylene gas main at the request of Tsuut&#39;ina Nation to accommodate a proposed Medical Centre currently in conflict with the existing gas main. The gas main will be installed at a depth of 0.8m - 1.1m below ground surface and will be installed primarily using horizontal diagonal drilling (HDD) and plow-in methods.

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling with original material, reclamation, and commissioning by flaring a small volume of sweet natural gas.

&nbsp;

One wetland (seasonal graminoid marsh) is located within 30m of the site project. The project will include the pre-construction wildlife sweeps.

The Pine View Golf Course proposes to demolish its clubhouse building. The building is located within the Pine View Golf Course at 1471 Blair Road in Ottawa. It has reached the end of its lifecycle and should be removed.

&nbsp;

The clubhouse is a one-storey concrete block building, with a suspected mid 1970s construction date. The building houses a dining hall/reception hall and restaurant, locker rooms, various offices, washrooms and mechanical rooms. It has a footprint of approximately 2,000 m3. There is no basement.


The building would be fully demolished. Some excavation would be required to remove the foundation. Vegetation immediately adjacent to the building may be selectively removed where required. The demolition would represent the first phase of a broader initiative, with the proponent intending to rebuild a new clubhouse in the future.

&nbsp;

The purpose of the project is to eliminate immediate and long-term health and safety risks and support the long-term stewardship and future use of the property.
&nbsp;

The proposed project would take place on lands owned by the NCC, and will be subject to a federal land use, design, and transaction approval under the National Capital Act. The NCC will make a determination under the Impact Assessment Act.


The construction area would be accessed with machines and equipment, and all work areas would be fenced accordingly. Construction would take place in the fall of 2026, outside the active nesting period for migratory birds. Construction work would follow all municipal by-laws, including those related to noise.
&nbsp;

Zagime Anishinabek is proposing a mix of both large and small format commercial tenants, medium and high-density mixed-use residential areas, and additional multi-unit residential buildings on the urban reserve of Saulteaux Junction Zagime IR No. 74-6. The proposed project is called Sage Meadow Development and will cover approximately 280 acres. Sage Meadow Development will utilize the existing systems on the property and tie in new service connections. The site is currently undeveloped agricultural land and project will be completed in phases. An existing gas station and two commercial buildings are located along the east side of the subject property. The remainder of the site consists of cultivated farmland.

&nbsp;

Phase1A will require roadwork and site preparation, trench excavation and backfill, pressure pipe domestic sewer, building services, utilities. Utilities currently identified on site are: Power lines, Buried electrical cable, buried or overhead phone cable, low pressure gas utility, buried sanitary sewer, buried storm sewer, buried water line.

&nbsp;

Approximately 1,190 m of roadways, including the reconstruction of Three Sisters Avenue to an urban collector cross-section, and intersection improvements at Dewdney Avenue and Pinkie Road, including turn lanes and traffic signals.

Star Blanket Cree Nation, Okanese First Nation, and Little Black Bear First Nation are proposing to construct a Waste Diversion Centre to serve the three member Nations.

&nbsp;

The development will include the construction of an attendant building with site equipment storage bays, along with an aggregate-surfaced pad area designed to accommodate diversion and storage bays for recyclable materials and household hazardous waste (including secondary containment). The site will also feature an elevated loading platform to facilitate transfer of refuse into waste containers. The constructed area will encompass approximately 2.5 ha.

&nbsp;

The proposed site is located within an abandoned yard site. There is no tree clearing required. Approximately 3.5 ha of topsoil stripping required, with ground disturbance activities consisting of excavation of perimeter ditching, building up the transfer pad area, and constructing the access road.

&nbsp;

Solid waste will primarily consist of packaging for building materials. Construction refuse will be confined to and collected from the site, with ultimate disposal to an off-reserve designated landfill facility. To manage surface water, perimeter ditching will be constructed around the entire site and will tie into existing natural drainage courses. Site access will be provided directly from Highway 617.

The Jacques Cartier Bridge is a vital roadway and active mobility link between Montr&eacute;al and Longueuil. To ensure user safety, maintain mobility in the metropolitan area, and preserve the durability of the infrastructure, The Jacques Cartier and Champlain Bridges Incorporated (JCCBI) plans to carry out road rehabilitation work across the entire Jacques Cartier Bridge in 2027 and 2028.

The project includes work on the Jacques Cartier Bridge, its approaches, and certain access ramps, covering a total area of around 80,000 m&sup2;.

Major work planned


	Milling and paving of sections 2 to 8 of the Jacques Cartier Bridge;
	Milling and paving of the Longueuil approach (Section 1);
	Milling and paving of the Montr&eacute;al approach (Section 9);
	Deck waterproofing work in Section 5 (&Icirc;le Sainte-H&eacute;l&egrave;ne Pavilion);
	Other replacement and installation work on the electrotechnical components;
	Other miscellaneous work (marking, drainage, etc.).


Work schedule

The work will begin in 2027 and is expected to take two (2) work seasons, namely 2027 and 2028.

Traffic management

To limit traffic hindrances, JCCBI will favour carrying out work requiring lane closures during weekend blitzes. This will involve closing certain lanes from Friday evening until Monday morning, while always keeping at least one lane open in each direction.

Nine (9) weekend blitzes are planned over the two (2) work seasons. Other short-term partial closures may also be required.

Environmental protection measures

The project includes the implementation of measures to protect the environment and limit adverse effects potentially caused by the work.
